// Bates, Glennerster, Gumede and Duflo, The Price is Wrong, Field Actions Science Reports,
// Special Issue 4 (2012), pp. 30-37; first published as a J-PAL Policy Bulletin, April 2011.
// Cites the 2012 reprint, whose text is CC BY; see README.md.
// Form: a conductive policy case. Ten randomized evaluations answer each rationale for charging
// (revenue, use, targeting, provider incentives) and each worry about giving products away
// (resentment, resale). The balance yields the verdict on small fees and the guidance on when to
// distribute for free, with a narrower lesson drawn from the one screening effect found.
// Linked: Worst of both worlds, What the ten experiments show, Subsidy level barely matters,
// Uniforms, What screening is worth, Benefits beyond the user, Prevention.
// Undercuts: Revenue at a cost (on Cost recovery), Better ways to motivate providers
// (on Provider incentives).

