# The policy conclusions {isGroup: true}

<Worst of both worlds>: Small fees fail on both counts at once, bringing in little money while costing a great deal of access.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "pp. 34, 36", note: "The lead lesson of the summary box (p. 34), repeated word for word as the first sentence of the policy lessons (p. 36). Linked: the verdict needs both failures, since either alone would make small fees a trade-off rather than the worst of both worlds."}

<Worst of both worlds>

(1) [Small fees raise little revenue]: small fees raise little revenue
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "pp. 34, 36", note: "Asserted rather than shown: the bulletin reports no revenue figures. Result 1 opens with the same claim, that small prices limit access 'without generating much revenue' (p. 31), and the nearest evidence is the deworming programme's cost per child treated, drawn under the answer to the cost-recovery case.", causes: {from: fee, to: revenue, sign: "+", basis: asserted}}
(2) [Small fees cut access for the poor]: Charging small fees dramatically reduces access to important products for the poor.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 30", note: "The title of the bulletin's first section, restated by its closing sentence. On p. 30 the other side of the debate is reported as pointing to the surges in take-up that followed the abolition of fees for schooling and health care; the bulletin's own case rests on the ten randomized evaluations instead.", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: study}}
-----
(3) [Small fees are the worst of both worlds]: Charging small fees in an attempt to balance access and “sustainability” may be the worst of both worlds #crux
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "pp. 34, 36", note: "The target is what p. 31 calls the common policy response to the competing aims of cost-sharing and free distribution, making products 'almost, but not quite, free'. Crux: the scare-quoted 'sustainability' is read as standing for the cost-sharing side's aims as a whole, including the promise that fees prevent waste, which is why the synthesis of results 1 to 3 is drawn as a second support. The narrower reading takes it as financial sustainability alone, as the two reasons given with it (revenue, access) suggest; on that reading the use and targeting results bear on whether to charge but not on this verdict."}

<What the ten experiments show>: Taken together, the evaluations suggest that even very small fees sharply limit access while doing little for use or targeting.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 36", note: "Linked: the synthesis is the conjunction of the first three results and fails if any one of them does."}

<What the ten experiments show>

(1) [Tiny fees sharply cut adoption]
(2) [Fees do not substantially promote use]
(3) [Fees are not a reliable way to target]
-----
(4) [Fees cut access without use or targeting gains]: charging even very small user fees often sharply limits access to health and education products and services without promoting use or encouraging better targeting to any useful extent
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", note: "What the ten experiments together are said to suggest, offered straight after the worst-of-both-worlds lesson is restated, as the answer to 'are user fees the answer?'. It is also what the next question calls the disadvantages of cost-sharing.", causes: [{from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: study}, {from: fee, to: use, sign: "0", basis: study}, {from: need, to: takeup, sign: "0", basis: study, given: ["when a fee is charged"]}]}

[Fees cut access without use or targeting gains]
    +> [Small fees are the worst of both worlds]
    +> [When to offer products free]

[Small fees are the worst of both worlds]
    <- <Cost recovery>
    <- [Other reasons to charge]
    <- [Acute care not addressed]: We know much less about the effect of user fees on take-up of treatment for acute illness. #conceded
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 37", note: "A scope limit the authors set on their own findings, drawn as an attack because Argdown has no relation for scope. The ten evaluations concern 'non-acute care' (p. 30), and the closing section says how much less is known about fees for treating acute illness. The J-PAL bulletin of 2011 also stated the limit among its lead lessons; the reprint's summary box leaves it out."}

[When to offer products free]: The disadvantages of cost-sharing are likely to be great enough that products should be offered for free when their benefits extend beyond the immediate user, when they are aimed mainly at preventive behavior, or when they are very cost-effective.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 36", source: "\"When are the disadvantages of cost-sharing likely to be so great that products should be offered for free?\"", note: "The bulletin's own question, answered by the three bullets under 'when to distribute for free'. The bullets are parts of this answer, each argued for, and are drawn as supports because Argdown has no part-whole relation. A conductive case: the considerations for it are independent, the concessions against it are the author's own, and the closing sentence, introduced by 'But', weighs them and finds them outweighed."}
    <+ [Fees impose a very high price on the poor]: the evidence summarized in this bulletin suggests that user fees, even small ones, are imposing a very high price on the poor and dramatically curtailing the potential benefits from primary education and highly effective preventive health products
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 37", note: "The bulletin's last sentence. It restates the title of the first section, and it answers the open questions listed just before it.", causes: [{from: fee, to: health, sign: "-", basis: study}, {from: fee, to: attend, sign: "-", basis: study}]}
    <+ [Individuals may not resent paying]
    <+ [Free recipients keep their bednets]
    <+ [Parents may underinvest in their children]: In situations where children benefit but parents have to pay user fees, there may similarly be a risk of underinvestment if parents do not fully take into account the benefits to the child.
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: asserted, hedged: true, given: ["where children benefit but parents have to pay user fees"], how: {actor: household, situation: "children benefit but parents have to pay user fees", response: "parents do not fully take into account the benefits to the child"}}}
    <+ [Free distribution matters most to the cash-poor]: to the extent that liquidity constraints (i.e. simple lack of ready cash) explain underinvestment, free distribution is particularly important for those with the most acute liquidity constraints, often the poor and women
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: asserted, given: ["those with the most acute liquidity constraints, often the poor and women"]}}
        <+ [Lack of cash explains part of the drop]
    <- [Cannot provide everything free]: governments and agencies cannot provide everything for free #conceded
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", note: "The concession that turns the question from whether to distribute for free into when. Drawn as an attack because it tells against free distribution as such; it is the reason this conclusion is conditional."}
    <- [Logistical issues remain]: Many difficult logistical issues remain for implementing systems of free distribution of cost-effective products for the poor. #conceded
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 37", note: "Followed by questions left to further evaluation: how to eliminate unofficial fees, how to stop health workers selling products meant to be free, whether providers' absenteeism rises once fees no longer supplement their incomes, and whether free clinics run out of stock more often."}
        <+ [Unofficial fees remain the norm]: In many cases where governments have announced free primary education or free healthcare for pregnant women and children, unofficial fees remain the norm. #conceded
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 37"}
        <+ [Health workers may sell free products]: health workers tasked with giving free health products to a particular target group (pregnant women, children, or the poor) might sell the products to others #conceded
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 33", note: "Called 'a bigger concern' than resale by recipients; ongoing research is said to be evaluating it.", causes: {from: fee, to: leak, sign: "-", basis: asserted, hedged: true}}

[Fees impose a very high price on the poor]
    <+ [Small fees cut access for the poor]
    <+ [The standard view is too simple]

# Small fees cut take-up {isGroup: true}

[Small fees cut access for the poor]
    <+ [Tiny fees sharply cut adoption]: Relative to free distribution, charging even very small user fees substantially reduces adoption.
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "pp. 30, 34", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: study}}
    <+ [For a six-dollar uniform children missed school]

[Tiny fees sharply cut adoption]
    <+ [Small price rises cut health take-up]: Even very small increases in price led to large drops in the number of people who chose to buy health products.
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31", note: "Result 1, called 'a consistent result' across the six studies of Figure 1.", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: study}}
        <+ [Deworming take-up fell from 75 to 19 percent]: The introduction of a small fee reduced deworming treatment from 75 percent in schools with free distribution to only 19 percent in schools with cost-sharing.
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31", note: "Study 1, Kremer and Miguel, Kenya. The fee averaged 30 cents and was introduced to make the programme more financially sustainable.", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: study}}
        <+ [Chlorine take-up fell over 30 points in Zambia]: Take-up fell by over 30 percentage points when prices increased from $0.09 to $0.25.
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31", note: "Study 6, Ashraf, Berry and Shapiro: dilute chlorine sold door to door.", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: study}}
        <+ [Chlorine use fell 52 points in Kenya]: Compared to free distribution, the percentage of households using chlorine in their water fell by 52 percentage points when households had to purchase the disinfectant.
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31", note: "Study 7, Kremer, Miguel, Null and Zwane; household water was tested for chlorine. The same study found little difference between half-price coupons and full price, so the fall comes between free and any price at all.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "-", basis: study}}
        <+ [Bednet sales fell 60 points at sixty cents]: bednet sales at prenatal clinics dropped by 60 percentage points when the price was increased from zero to $0.60
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31", note: "Study 2, Cohen and Dupas, Kenya. Sixty cents is still 15 cents below what social marketing programmes typically charge pregnant women there.", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: study}}
    <+ [Demand is insensitive to the subsidy level]

<Subsidy level barely matters>: A 90 percent subsidy lost as much demand as subsidies of half or nothing, so demand does not track the exact subsidy level.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 31", note: "Linked: the comparison needs both subsidy rates and the matching slopes. It bears on the 'almost, but not quite, free' policy directly, since a deep subsidy does not keep the takers a free product has."}

<Subsidy level barely matters>

(1) [A sixty-cent bednet is a 90 percent subsidy]: A bednet costs $6, so a price of $0.60 represents a 90 percent subsidy rate.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31"}
(2) [Chlorine subsidies ran from half to none]: The prices charged for water disinfectant in Kenya, by contrast, ranged from a 50 percent subsidy to no subsidy at all.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31"}
(3) [Bednet demand fell as steeply as chlorine demand]: the drop in demand for bednets is about as steep as the drop in demand for water disinfectant
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31"}
-----
(4) [Demand is insensitive to the subsidy level]: demand does not appear to be sensitive to the exact subsidy level
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 31", causes: {from: subsidy, to: takeup, sign: "0", basis: study}}

<Uniforms>: Where school fees are gone a six-dollar uniform is still a main cost of school, and giving it free raised attendance, so a small price keeps some children from a large public investment.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 32", note: "Studies 9 (Evans, Kremer and Ngatia) and 10 (Duflo, Dupas, Kremer and Sinei), both in Kenya. Attendance rose 6.4 percentage points among primary pupils given a free uniform, and 14-year-old girls were 2.5 points less likely to drop out."}

<Uniforms>

(1) [A uniform is a main remaining cost of school]: In Kenya, primary school fees have been eliminated, and a $6 uniform presents one of the main remaining costs of attending school.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32"}
(2) [Free uniforms raised attendance]: In both evaluations, students randomly selected to receive a free uniform were more likely to attend school
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32", causes: {from: fee, to: attend, sign: "-", basis: study}}
(3) [A uniform is a small share of what schooling costs]: the cost of a uniform represents only a small fraction of the cost of schooling, considering that the Kenyan government has already invested significant funds to operate and staff schools #crux
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32", note: "Crux. Read as the author's reply to the concession just before it: the smaller drop for education is no comfort, because the uniform is a trivial part of what schooling costs and the government has paid the rest, so each child it keeps away wastes that investment. The other reading takes the sentence as explaining why the drop is smaller (a uniform is a small price on the whole good), which would set it beside the concession rather than under the reply. The following 'Still' fits either."}
-----
(4) [For a six-dollar uniform children missed school]: Still, for the sake of a $6 uniform, 2.5 to 6.4 percent of children were not attending school.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32", causes: {from: fee, to: attend, sign: "-", basis: study}}

[For a six-dollar uniform children missed school]
    <- [The drop looks smaller for education]: At face value, the drop in take-up between free provision and cost-sharing appears to be much smaller for education than for the health products outlined in Figure 1. #conceded
