# Fees do not buy use {isGroup: true}

[Fees do not substantially promote use]: fees do not substantially promote use
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32", note: "Result 2's heading; the section's verdict is 'The balance of evidence is that it does not.' The section grants the concern behind fees ('It is a waste of resources to hand out products for free if they will not be used correctly'), and 'substantially' leaves room for the one screening effect found. Use was checked by home visits and chlorine tests, so the usage figures are lower bounds.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "0", basis: study}}
    <+ [Usage no different in Uganda]: researchers found no difference in usage between those who received free long-lasting insecticidal bednets and those who received cash to buy bednets
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 33", note: "Study 5, Hoffmann, Barrett and Just.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "0", basis: study}}
    <+ [Usage no different in Kenya]: usage was no different after two months and after one year between people who received vouchers for a free bednet and those who received vouchers to purchase a subsidized bednet
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 33", note: "Study 4, Dupas.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "0", basis: study, lag: "two months and one year"}}
    <+ [No evidence that paying itself promotes use]: There is no evidence that the act of paying for a product makes a recipient more likely to use it.
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "pp. 30, 34", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "0", basis: study}}
        <+ [Neither effect for bednets in Kenya]: there was neither a screening effect nor a psychological effect of paying for bednets
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "Study 2, Cohen and Dupas. To separate the effects, a random subset of those who had already chosen to buy at a price was then offered a further discount or the product free.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "0", basis: study}}
        <+ [No commitment effect for chlorine in Zambia]: researchers did not observe a psychological commitment effect of paying for water disinfectant
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "Study 6. Those willing to pay who were then given the chlorine free used it as much as those who paid.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "0", basis: study}}
        <- [Paying itself encourages use]: the mere act of paying for something encourages people to use it more than if they had received the same product for free #reported
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 33", note: "The psychological commitment or sunk-cost effect, attributed to many NGOs and agencies. In the summary box (p. 34) it is the common claim that people are more likely to use what they have sacrificed for, and part of the social entrepreneurs' case.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "+", basis: asserted, how: {actor: household, situation: "has paid for the product", habit: "rationalize their purchases", response: "uses it more"}}}
    <+ [Screening also turned away would-be users]: Charging did screen out some people who were always unlikely to use the product, although it also screened out some who would have used it, had the chlorine been free.
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", causes: [{from: fee, to: use, sign: "+", selects: true, basis: study}, {from: fee, to: use, sign: "-", basis: study, given: ["those who would have used it, had the chlorine been free"]}]}
    <+ [Fees cannot raise use where taking is using]: For these products, where there is no step between take-up and use, there is no potential for user fees to increase use.
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32", note: "Deworming pills swallowed under a teacher's eye, injections given by a health worker. Only products needing repeated, active use leave fees any room to matter.", causes: [{from: fee, to: use, sign: "0", basis: asserted, given: ["products where there is no step between take-up and use"]}, {from: takeup, to: use, sign: "+", basis: asserted, given: ["products where there is no step between take-up and use"]}]}
    <- [Fees screen out those unlikely to use]: a user fee may help to prevent waste by screening out those who are not likely to use the product #reported
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 33", note: "The screening effect, one of two ways charging 'has been thought to promote use', resting on the premise that 'individuals who value a product highly may be both more likely to pay for a product and more likely to use it once they have it'.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "+", selects: true, hedged: true, basis: asserted}}
    <- [The screening effect existed in Zambia]

<What screening is worth>: A screening effect was found, but what it is worth depends on what becomes of product taken and not used, so recipients' other uses should weigh in deciding what to prioritize for free distribution.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 34"}

<What screening is worth>

(1) [The screening effect existed in Zambia]: A 10 percent increase in the price of the disinfectant led to a 3 to 4 percent increase in the probability that the eventual owners of the disinfectant would be found using it #conceded
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "Study 6, Ashraf, Berry and Shapiro; introduced with 'The screening effect, however, did exist'.", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "+", selects: true, basis: study}}
(2) [The worth of screening turns on unused bottles]: The importance of this screening effect depends heavily on what happens to the bottles of chlorine that people accept or buy, but do not ultimately use in their drinking water.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34"}
(3) [Stockpilers stop taking more bottles]: If people accept chlorine bottles, intending to use them in their drinking water, but do not immediately use these bottles, they are unlikely to continue accepting more and more bottles in subsequent rounds of free or subsidized distribution.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", causes: {from: takeup, to: later, sign: "-", basis: asserted, given: ["people accept chlorine bottles, intending to use them in their drinking water, but do not immediately use these bottles"]}}
-- {uses: [1, 2, 3]} --
(4) [Screening helps less in the long term]: the screening effect of charging would be less useful in reducing waste in the long-term
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34"}
(5) [Screening helps more where products serve other uses]: If people are accepting the chlorine to use it for less socially important purposes (such as cleaning), the screening effect of pricing may be more useful for achieving the more socially important health benefits.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", causes: {from: fee, to: use, sign: "+", selects: true, hedged: true, basis: asserted, given: ["people are accepting the chlorine to use it for less socially important purposes (such as cleaning)"]}}
-- {uses: [2, 4, 5]} --
(6) [Other uses should weigh in prioritizing free distribution]: Whether recipients tend to use health products for other (less socially important) purposes should be a factor in deciding whether to prioritize a product for free distribution.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "Offered as 'an important general point' and argued for on its own, so drawn as a conclusion in its own right rather than folded into the guidance on when to distribute for free."}

# Fees do not target need {isGroup: true}

[Fees are not a reliable way to target]: charging fees is not generally a reliable way to help target health products to those who need them most
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "Result 3's conclusion from five studies. The bulletin also sets out the opposite possibility, that if those who need a product most are poorer and less able to pay, charging may worsen targeting; it does not conclude for it.", causes: {from: need, to: takeup, sign: "0", basis: study, given: ["when a fee is charged"]}}
    <+ [Heavy worm loads did not raise willingness to pay]: children with high parasitic worm loads would have benefited most from deworming treatment, but their families were no more likely to pay for treatment than the families of children with low worm loads
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "Study 1, Kenya.", causes: {from: need, to: takeup, sign: "0", basis: study, given: ["when a fee is charged"]}}
    <+ [Bednet buyers were no sicker]: pregnant women who were willing to pay higher prices for bednets appeared no sicker (in terms of measured anemia) than the average prenatal client when they made their purchase
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "Study 2, Kenya. Malaria in pregnancy can cause anemia.", causes: {from: need, to: takeup, sign: "0", basis: study, given: ["when a fee is charged"]}}
    <+ [More young children meant lower willingness to pay in Uganda]: households with more young children actually had a lower willingness to pay for bednets
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "Study 5, Uganda, although families with young children have the greater need for bednets.", causes: {from: need, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: study, given: ["when a fee is charged"]}}
    <+ [Families with young children bought no more chlorine]: Families with children under five are not more likely to buy water disinfectant
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "The summary box's account (p. 34) of studies 6 and 7. On p. 35, families with more young children were not willing to pay more for chlorine in Zambia, and no more likely to buy it subsidized in Kenya.", causes: {from: need, to: takeup, sign: "0", basis: study, given: ["when a fee is charged"]}}
    <- [Charging may target the neediest]: charging may be a convenient way to target subsidized products to the most needy #reported
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "A hypothesis set out conditionally: if those who stand to benefit more know it and are therefore more willing to pay. On p. 30 it is the question whether fees target products to those who need them most.", causes: {from: need, to: takeup, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true, given: ["when a fee is charged", "If they are aware of this fact and thus are more willing to pay for these products"]}}

# The feared costs of giving products away {isGroup: true}

[Individuals may not resent paying]: individuals may not resent having to pay after having received a product for free in the past
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "Hedged by the author ('these results suggest'). The policy lessons put it as free distribution not necessarily undermining future willingness to buy (p. 36).", causes: {from: fee, to: later, sign: "0", basis: study, hedged: true, lag: "one year"}}
    <+ [Free distribution can teach the benefits of a product]: learning about the benefits of a product through free distribution may actually make people more willing to pay for a product in the future
