# Revenue and other reasons to charge {isGroup: true}

<Cost recovery>: User fees have long been advocated as a way to help recover costs and make programs more financially sustainable. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "The oldest case for fees. On p. 30 they are said to have been advocated to promote the sustainability of health services, to keep goods from being wasted, and to give frontline services flexible revenue.", causes: [{from: fee, to: revenue, sign: "+", basis: asserted}, {from: revenue, to: supply, sign: "+", basis: asserted}]}

<Revenue at a cost>: Because fees cut take-up, the cost of running a program falls on far fewer users, so money collected through fees need not make the program more cost-effective for society.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "An undercut, not an attack. The bulletin grants the premise ('Charging may generate some revenue to help cover program costs') and denies that it makes the program better, since 'the revenue generated under cost-sharing comes at a cost to the poor'. The shift is from the implementing organization's accounts to society's."}

<Revenue at a cost>

(1) [Fewer users mean higher cost per person]: if charging small amounts significantly reduces take-up, the cost of administrating the program will be amortized over far fewer users, increasing the administrative costs per person
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", causes: {from: takeup, to: unitcost, sign: "-", basis: asserted}}
(2) [Tiny fees sharply cut adoption]
(3) [Deworming cost per child more than doubled]: the cost per child dewormed under cost-sharing was more than twice as high as under free distribution ($4.26 vs. $1.48), and far fewer children received the treatment
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "Study 1, Kenya.", causes: {from: fee, to: unitcost, sign: "+", basis: study}}
-----
(4) [Fees need not raise cost-effectiveness]: collecting money through user fees will not necessarily increase the cost-effectiveness of a program when one considers the costs and benefits from a societal perspective, rather than from the perspective of the organization implementing the program
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", causes: {from: fee, to: costeff, sign: "0", basis: study}}

[Fees need not raise cost-effectiveness]
    <+ [Cost-sharing at best marginally more cost-effective for bednets]: Cohen and Dupas find cost-sharing for bednets in Kenya at best marginally more cost-effective than free distribution, and suggest that free distribution could save many more lives.
        {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "p. 35", source: "\"find cost-sharing to be at best marginally more cost-effective than free distribution, but suggest that free distribution could save many more lives\"", note: "Study 2.", causes: [{from: fee, to: costeff, sign: "0", basis: study}, {from: fee, to: health, sign: "-", basis: study, hedged: true}]}

<Cost recovery>
    <_ <Revenue at a cost>

[Small fees raise little revenue]
    <- [Small fees can fund sustainable distribution]: small fees can help fund marketing networks that bring socially important products to the poor in a sustainable way #reported
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 30", note: "The social entrepreneurs' case. The bulletin answers it only through the worst-of-both-worlds argument itself; no finding it reports concerns marketing networks.", causes: [{from: fee, to: revenue, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true}, {from: revenue, to: supply, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true}, {from: supply, to: takeup, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true}]}

<Provider incentives>: Fees may give service providers a reason to keep supplies in stock and come to work, which would be a reason to charge that the evaluations did not test. #conceded
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "pp. 34, 37", note: "Conceded and left open. In the summary box (p. 34) 'the importance of these potential effects needs rigorous evaluation'; on p. 37 'These effects have yet to be tested by randomized evaluations'."}

<Provider incentives>

(1) [Fees may motivate providers]: User fees may incentivize service providers to stock supplies and come to work #conceded
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "On p. 37, as supplements to salaries, fees could give providers incentives to keep products in stock, replenish supplies, and come to work.", causes: {from: fee, to: effort, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true}}
