# Why small prices deter so many {isGroup: true}

[The standard view is too simple]: a number of pieces of evidence suggest that this is too simplified a story
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "'This' is the standard economics view drawn as attacking it. What is at stake: if low willingness to pay meant low value, those whom fees deter would lose little."}
    <+ [Reluctant to pay yet unwilling to sell]: people were both reluctant to pay much for a bednet and yet were unwilling to sell it for a much higher price
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35"}
        <+ [Most would not sell a free net at cost]
    <+ [Lack of cash explains part of the drop]: a lack of cash on hand explains at least part of the drop in demand seen with user fees
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", note: "Hedged ('This suggests'). A test of whether the effort of deliberating deters purchases (study 8, Spears, India) found a small effect that 'at best' explains a small part of the price sensitivity, and the section closes by allowing that 'there may still be debate about why we see this behavior' while holding that very small price increases deter many.", causes: {from: cash, to: takeup, sign: "+", basis: study}}
        <+ [Cash in hand more than doubled willingness to pay]: individuals using their own cash were willing to pay on average $2.34 for a bednet. When the researchers provided people with enough cash to buy a net, the individuals were willing to pay more than twice that amount ($5.94).
            {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "Study 5, Hoffmann, Barrett and Just, Uganda.", causes: {from: cash, to: takeup, sign: "+", basis: study}}
