# When to distribute for free {isGroup: true}

<Benefits beyond the user>: Where use of a product benefits others, individuals will not pay for those benefits, so giving it away yields more social benefit than charging.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "pp. 32, 36", note: "The first bullet under 'when to distribute for free', with its middle premise from the spillovers box (p. 32), which draws the same lesson: 'Goods and services with sufficiently high positive spillovers should be a priority for free distribution.' One kind of spillover listed, inspiring neighbours to adopt new technology, is what result 4 found among the neighbours of free bednet recipients; the bulletin does not draw that link itself."}

<Benefits beyond the user>

(1) [Health and education investments benefit the community]: Many investments in education and health have additional benefits to the community associated with widespread individual use.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", causes: {from: use, to: social, sign: "+", basis: asserted}}
(2) [People will not pay for benefits to others]: individuals may be unwilling to pay for the benefits they create for others
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32", causes: {from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: asserted, hedged: true, given: ["where individual use of a product creates positive spillovers"], how: {actor: household, situation: "the benefits they create for others", response: "unwilling to pay for them"}}}
-- {uses: [1, 2]} --
(3) [Giving away yields more social benefit]: In cases where these benefits to the community are large, distributing these products for free can lead to a larger social benefit than charging.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", causes: {from: fee, to: social, sign: "-", basis: asserted, hedged: true, given: ["where these benefits to the community are large"]}}
-- {uses: [1, 3]} --
(4) [Products with spillovers are candidates for free distribution]: products which reduce the prevalence or transmission of diseases, which might inspire neighbors to adopt beneficial new technology, which boost the productivity of others, or which otherwise have benefits beyond its users are good candidates for free distribution
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36"}

[Health and education investments benefit the community]
    <+ [Deworming benefits untreated children]: When some children are treated for parasitic worms, even untreated children in the same school and in nearby schools benefit
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32", note: "Cited to Miguel and Kremer 2004: lower worm loads and better attendance, because deworming breaks the cycle of transmission.", causes: {from: use, to: social, sign: "+", basis: study}}

[Products with spillovers are candidates for free distribution]
    +> [When to offer products free]

<Prevention>: People underinvest in prevention, so pricing that helps them make up-front preventive investments may pay off especially well.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "p. 36", note: "The second bullet, headed 'When products and services are aimed mainly at preventive behavior.' Premise 2 comes from the section on convenience (p. 36), where the concern about underinvestment is raised; joining it to this bullet is the reconstruction's."}

