# The mechanism, as the text states it {isGroup: true}

[Unused products prevent nothing]: "A bednet is no help in preventing malaria if it is still in its package and not hung up. Chlorine does not prevent diarrhea if it is not regularly added to a family’s drinking water."
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 32", causes: {from: use, to: health, sign: "+", basis: asserted}}

[Revenue may help cover costs]: "Charging may generate some revenue to help cover program costs, but it is important to realize that the revenue generated under cost-sharing comes at a cost to the poor."
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "Granted in the box on cost recovery; the rest of the sentence turns to what the revenue costs the poor, which the box goes on to count as cost per child.", causes: [{from: fee, to: revenue, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true}, {from: revenue, to: supply, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true}]}

[Higher cost per person, lower cost-effectiveness]: A program whose cost per person reached rises is, other things equal, less cost-effective.
    {fidelity: "imputation", warrant: "enthymeme", pinpoint: "p. 35", note: "The inference of the box on cost recovery runs from costs amortized over far fewer users to fees not raising cost-effectiveness, and needs this step; the text does not state it.", causes: {from: unitcost, to: costeff, sign: "-", basis: asserted}}

[Charging may worsen targeting]: "if those who need a product most are also poorer and less able to afford fees, charging may actually lead to worse targeting"
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 34", note: "Set out beside the rival hypothesis that charging targets the needy; the text concludes for neither, though the Ugandan result points this way.", causes: {from: need, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: asserted, hedged: true, given: ["when a fee is charged", "if those who need a product most are also poorer and less able to afford fees"]}}

[Neighbours saw the benefits of nets]: "Free distribution meant people had more neighbors with nets, so it is possible that they had greater exposure to the benefits of the nets and thus were more likely to purchase one."
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 35", causes: {from: takeup, to: nbuy, sign: "+", basis: asserted, hedged: true, how: {actor: community, situation: "more neighbors with nets", habit: "greater exposure to the benefits of the nets", response: "more likely to purchase one"}}}

[Present bias may explain underinvestment]: "Behavioral economics has focused attention on one potential explanation, present bias, where immediate concerns trump long-term factors."
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", note: "Offered for underinvestment in time and money alike, so marked on both the fee's step and the distance step.", causes: [{from: fee, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: testimony, hedged: true, how: {actor: household, habit: "present bias, where immediate concerns trump long-term factors", response: "underinvest (both in time and money) in preventive healthcare"}}, {from: distance, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: testimony, hedged: true, how: {actor: household, habit: "present bias, where immediate concerns trump long-term factors", response: "underinvest (both in time and money) in preventive healthcare"}}]}

[Free makes the decision simpler]: "If a product or service is free, however, the calculation becomes much simpler–there are no costs, only benefits– and people may be more likely to take the good."
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "p. 36", note: "The idea study 8 (Spears, India) tested: that the effort of thinking about a purchase may deter people.", causes: [{from: fee, to: deliberate, sign: "+", basis: asserted}, {from: deliberate, to: takeup, sign: "-", basis: asserted, hedged: true}]}

